Deep in the night, it dampens silk slippers.
I draw down the crystal curtain—
Through its clear shimmer, I gaze at the autumn moon.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"The young wife, sheltered in her chamber, knows no sorrow;
On a spring day, dressed with care, she climbs the green tower.
Then suddenly she sees the willows leafing along the road—
And regrets urging her husband to seek a marquis’s rank.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"Hear not the sound of rain piercing leaves and striking them;
Why not chant and whistle, and walk on slowly?
A bamboo staff and straw sandals carry me more lightly than a horse.
Who’s afraid? In a cloak of mist and rain, I’ll let it shape my whole life.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"Beside Vermilion Bird Bridge, wildflowers bloom;
At Black-clad Lane, the setting sun slants low.
The swallows once nesting in Wang and Xie halls
Now fly into the homes of common folk.","notes":["Wang and Xie were prominent aristocratic families of the Eastern Jin dynasty."]}

{"translation":"Wildflowers bloom beside Sparrow Bridge;
the setting sun slants over Black Robe Alley.
Swallows once beneath the eaves of Wang and Xie
now fly into the homes of ordinary people.","notes":["Wang and Xie were prominent aristocratic families of the past."]}

{"translation":"White dew gathers on the jade steps;
Deep night soaks the silk stockings.
The crystal curtain is lowered—
Through its luminous veil, the autumn moon is gazed upon.","notes":["The poem leaves the person who gazes, and who lowers the curtain, unstated."]}

{"translation":"The young wife, sheltered in her rooms, knows no sorrow;
On a spring day, dressed with care, she climbs the green tower.
Then, on the roadside, she glimpses willows bright with spring—
And regrets she urged her husband to seek a marquis’ rank.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"Since northern horsemen came to threaten the river,
The ruined pools and towering trees
Still dread the mention of war.
Dusk slowly gathers;
A clear horn sounds the cold—
All through the empty city.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"White dew gathers on the jade steps;  
deep in the night, it soaks her silk stockings.  
She turns back and lowers the crystal curtain,  
gazing through its delicate shimmer at the autumn moon.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"Birds call for skies to clear;
at daybreak, by the eaves, they peer and chatter.
The first sun dries last night’s rain from the leaves.
On the water, clear and round,
the lotus leaves rise, one by one,
lifted by the wind.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"Hand in hand, we gaze through tear-blurred eyes;
At last, no words—only a choked silence.
I think of the miles ahead, the mist-veiled waters,
The dusk haze deepening beneath Chu’s vast sky.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"All the birds have flown high and vanished;
A lone cloud drifts leisurely away.
We gaze at each other, never weary—
Only Jingting Mountain and I.","notes":[]}

{"translation":"At dawn, the travel bells stir;
I set forth, sorrowing for home.
Cockcrow, a thatched inn, the moon;
footprints mark the plank bridge in frost.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain road;
citrus blossoms brighten the courier wall.
Thinking then of my Duling dream—
wild ducks and geese fill the returning pond.","notes":[]}

